1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,690
A pin on a map.

2
00:00:01,690 --> 00:00:03,760
A seat number in an exam hall.

3
00:00:03,760 --> 00:00:06,947
A treasure map saying ten
steps east, five north.

4
00:00:07,347 --> 00:00:10,418
Three different things,
all answering one question.

5
00:00:10,418 --> 00:00:11,797
Where exactly is it?

6
00:00:12,247 --> 00:00:15,860
Aarav parked his bicycle somewhere
in the big college ground.

7
00:00:15,860 --> 00:00:17,861
His friend asks, where is it?

8
00:00:18,211 --> 00:00:20,573
Aarav points and says, over there.

9
00:00:20,573 --> 00:00:21,583
But how far?

10
00:00:21,583 --> 00:00:22,691
And which way?

11
00:00:23,141 --> 00:00:25,607
Physics answers both with just one arrow.

12
00:00:25,607 --> 00:00:28,881
By the end of this video,
you will draw it yourself.

13
00:00:29,381 --> 00:00:31,210
So what is a position vector?

14
00:00:31,560 --> 00:00:35,214
First, pick one fixed point
to measure from, the origin O.

15
00:00:35,214 --> 00:00:36,800
Here, the college gate.

16
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:38,954
Next, draw two axes.

17
00:00:38,954 --> 00:00:40,830
The x axis points east.

18
00:00:40,830 --> 00:00:42,761
The y axis points north.

19
00:00:43,161 --> 00:00:46,754
Now draw an arrow from the
origin straight to the bicycle.

20
00:00:46,754 --> 00:00:49,225
That arrow is the position vector, r.

21
00:00:49,675 --> 00:00:51,652
Its length tells you how far.

22
00:00:51,652 --> 00:00:53,870
Its direction tells you which way.

23
00:00:53,870 --> 00:00:55,646
Both answers in one arrow.

24
00:00:56,146 --> 00:01:00,653
Using i hat and j hat, r
equals x i hat plus y j hat.

25
00:01:00,653 --> 00:01:04,050
Here, twenty i hat plus ten j hat metres.

26
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:06,205
Now watch it live.

27
00:01:06,205 --> 00:01:09,506
Aarav rides from P to
Q along a curved path.

28
00:01:09,856 --> 00:01:13,890
At P, he is twenty metres east
and ten metres north of the gate.

29
00:01:14,290 --> 00:01:18,763
As he rides, the position arrow
swings and stretches, always from O to

30
00:01:18,763 --> 00:01:19,720
the bicycle.

31
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:21,252
Watch the graph.

32
00:01:21,252 --> 00:01:22,429
x grows steadily.

33
00:01:22,429 --> 00:01:24,508
y climbs fast, then levels off.

34
00:01:24,958 --> 00:01:25,922
Slow motion.

35
00:01:25,922 --> 00:01:26,822
Freeze.

36
00:01:26,577 --> 00:01:31,088
Right now, r is the arrow from O
to the bicycle, at this instant.

37
00:01:31,588 --> 00:01:35,022
He stops at Q, fifty metres
east and fifty north.

38
00:01:35,422 --> 00:01:36,801
Now the big idea.

39
00:01:36,801 --> 00:01:39,404
Draw one arrow straight from P to Q.

40
00:01:39,404 --> 00:01:41,884
This is the displacement, delta r.

41
00:01:42,284 --> 00:01:43,418
The road curved.

42
00:01:43,418 --> 00:01:47,348
But the displacement is one straight
arrow, from start to finish.

43
00:01:47,848 --> 00:01:52,243
Let's find delta r from the two
position arrows, one step at a time.

44
00:01:52,593 --> 00:01:53,493
Step one.

45
00:01:53,417 --> 00:01:55,911
The position at the start is r one.

46
00:01:55,911 --> 00:01:58,304
The position at the end is r two.

47
00:01:59,054 --> 00:01:59,972
Step two.

48
00:01:59,972 --> 00:02:03,177
O to P, then P to Q, gets you to Q.

49
00:02:03,177 --> 00:02:06,022
So r one plus delta r equals r two.

50
00:02:06,772 --> 00:02:07,911
Step three.

51
00:02:07,911 --> 00:02:09,632
Move r one across.

52
00:02:09,632 --> 00:02:12,579
Delta r equals r two minus r one.

53
00:02:12,579 --> 00:02:14,350
Final minus initial.

54
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:16,098
Step four.

55
00:02:16,098 --> 00:02:20,791
In parts, delta r equals delta
x i hat plus delta y j hat.

56
00:02:21,541 --> 00:02:22,687
Remember this.

57
00:02:22,687 --> 00:02:26,851
Displacement is the final position
minus the initial position.

58
00:02:27,551 --> 00:02:29,283
Now a surprising question.

59
00:02:29,283 --> 00:02:31,626
What if we pick a different origin?

60
00:02:31,976 --> 00:02:34,220
On the left, the origin is the gate.

61
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:36,243
On the right, it is the canteen.

62
00:02:36,643 --> 00:02:39,159
From the canteen, r one is a new arrow.

63
00:02:39,159 --> 00:02:40,859
r two is a new arrow too.

64
00:02:41,259 --> 00:02:45,696
But both arrows changed by the same
extra piece, the arrow from the canteen

65
00:02:45,696 --> 00:02:46,661
to the gate.

66
00:02:47,111 --> 00:02:49,879
When we subtract, that
extra piece cancels out.

67
00:02:50,379 --> 00:02:51,909
So, pause and think.

68
00:02:51,909 --> 00:02:56,516
With the origin at the canteen, does
delta r change, or stay the same?

69
00:02:59,716 --> 00:03:01,238
It stays the same!

70
00:03:01,238 --> 00:03:04,293
Thirty i hat plus forty j hat from both.

71
00:03:04,293 --> 00:03:06,592
Position depends on the origin.

72
00:03:06,592 --> 00:03:08,266
Displacement does not.

73
00:03:08,816 --> 00:03:09,905
Side by side.

74
00:03:09,905 --> 00:03:14,008
A position vector starts at the
origin and ends at the object.

75
00:03:14,008 --> 00:03:16,336
Move the origin, and it changes.

76
00:03:16,736 --> 00:03:21,040
A displacement starts where the
motion starts and ends where it ends.

77
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:23,397
It ignores the origin and the path.

78
00:03:23,997 --> 00:03:25,083
Now you try.

79
00:03:25,083 --> 00:03:29,155
Aarav rides a full loop and
parks exactly where he started.

80
00:03:29,155 --> 00:03:30,990
What is his displacement?

81
00:03:30,990 --> 00:03:32,340
Pause and decide.

82
00:03:35,540 --> 00:03:36,440
Zero!

83
00:03:36,120 --> 00:03:40,083
He ended where he began, so
r two minus r one is zero.

84
00:03:40,083 --> 00:03:43,312
The loop's length is
distance, not displacement.

85
00:03:43,962 --> 00:03:45,697
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:46,047 --> 00:03:51,058
A ball moves from A, one comma two,
to B, six comma fourteen, in metres.

87
00:03:51,058 --> 00:03:52,547
Find its displacement.

88
00:03:53,047 --> 00:03:55,508
Delta x equals six minus one.

89
00:03:55,508 --> 00:03:56,667
Five metres.

90
00:03:57,417 --> 00:04:00,034
Delta y equals fourteen minus two.

91
00:04:00,034 --> 00:04:01,246
Twelve metres.

92
00:04:01,996 --> 00:04:06,402
So delta r equals five i hat
plus twelve j hat metres.

93
00:04:07,152 --> 00:04:11,105
Its size is the root of five
squared plus twelve squared.

94
00:04:11,105 --> 00:04:12,671
Root one sixty nine.

95
00:04:12,671 --> 00:04:13,901
Thirteen metres.

96
00:04:14,651 --> 00:04:19,621
Direction: tan theta is twelve over
five, so theta is about sixty seven

97
00:04:19,621 --> 00:04:20,521
degrees.

98
00:04:21,041 --> 00:04:22,507
Now, the exam trap.

99
00:04:22,857 --> 00:04:27,598
Many students subtract the wrong way
round, and write delta r as r one

100
00:04:27,598 --> 00:04:28,686
minus r two.

101
00:04:29,036 --> 00:04:32,581
That arrow points backwards,
from the end to the start.

102
00:04:32,581 --> 00:04:34,344
Always final minus initial.

103
00:04:34,344 --> 00:04:35,734
r two minus r one.

104
00:04:36,384 --> 00:04:37,808
Where is this asked?

105
00:04:37,808 --> 00:04:42,120
As a quick calculation: find the
displacement from two position vectors,

106
00:04:42,120 --> 00:04:43,153
and its size.

107
00:04:43,653 --> 00:04:44,908
Exam level now.

108
00:04:44,908 --> 00:04:50,080
A particle moves from r one to r two
along a curved path ten metres long.

109
00:04:50,080 --> 00:04:53,027
What is the magnitude of its displacement?

110
00:04:53,027 --> 00:04:54,336
Pause and solve.

111
00:04:59,736 --> 00:05:01,118
The answer is C.

112
00:05:01,118 --> 00:05:02,212
Seven metres.

113
00:05:02,712 --> 00:05:08,274
Delta r is r two minus r one, which
is two i hat, minus six j hat, plus

114
00:05:08,274 --> 00:05:09,424
three k hat.

115
00:05:09,924 --> 00:05:13,822
Its size is the square root of
four plus thirty six plus nine.

116
00:05:13,822 --> 00:05:15,813
The square root of forty nine.

117
00:05:15,813 --> 00:05:16,713
Seven.

118
00:05:16,889 --> 00:05:19,621
Ten is the path length, the distance.

119
00:05:19,621 --> 00:05:22,020
Eleven adds parts without squaring.

120
00:05:22,020 --> 00:05:24,752
Two point one just subtracts the sizes.

121
00:05:24,752 --> 00:05:25,659
All traps.

122
00:05:26,259 --> 00:05:27,723
Here is your recap.

123
00:05:27,723 --> 00:05:29,228
Screenshot this card.

124
00:05:32,428 --> 00:05:35,264
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

125
00:05:35,664 --> 00:05:36,564
One.

126
00:05:36,144 --> 00:05:40,078
Why do we need an origin before
we can write a position vector?

127
00:05:40,978 --> 00:05:41,878
Two.

128
00:05:41,519 --> 00:05:45,276
Why does moving the origin
not change the displacement?

129
00:05:46,176 --> 00:05:47,076
Three.

130
00:05:46,806 --> 00:05:49,936
Can displacement be zero
when distance is not?

131
00:05:49,936 --> 00:05:51,207
Give an example.

132
00:05:52,307 --> 00:05:55,944
Next time: one slanted arrow
hides two straight arrows.

133
00:05:55,944 --> 00:05:59,330
We find them in vector
components in two dimensions.
